agent's closing message:
I've written about 280 requirements for the rear lamp across four files in `/Users/imantsliepins/Personal/bench-out/agent-dev/`. I couldn't run anything to check that the JSON parses or that the files merge, so please validate them before submitting.

| File | IDs | Regulations covered |
|---|---|---|
| `submission-r148.json` | R-001 to R-081 | UN R148: the current regulation for the position, stop and reversing lamps (application, markings, construction, light sources, photometry, colour, light distribution, warm-up behaviour, production conformity) |
| `submission-r7-r23.json` | R-101 to R-144, R-201 to R-220 | UN R7 (older position and stop lamp regulation) and UN R23 (older reversing lamp regulation) |
| `submission-retro.json` | R-301 to R-334, R-401 to R-443 | UN R3 (older retro-reflector regulation) and UN R150 (current one): Class IA/IB markings, construction, shape, colour, reflection values, heat, water, dust, corrosion, fuel, oil and weathering tests, production conformity |
| `submission-r48.json` | R-501 to R-563, R-601 to R-609, R-701 to R-703 | UN R48 vehicle installation, UN R10 EMC for the LED electronics, UN R26 external projections |

The R48 part covers colour limits, symmetry of the left/right pair, combined functions, red light not visible from the front, movable parts such as a tailgate, supply voltage, and the number, position, visibility angles, orientation, wiring and tell-tales of each function.

Choices that affect how this will be scored:

- **Old and new regulations both included.** R48 allows approval under either the old regulations (R7, R23, R3) or the current ones (R148, R150). Since recall counts four times precision, I listed both, so many obligations appear twice under different citations.
- **Conditional items included.** Several requirements apply only if the design has a certain feature, and each is worded with its condition. Examples are replaceable light-source modules, variable intensity, a lamp marked "D", a manufacturer logo, mounting on a movable part, and the alternative water spray test for a retro-reflector inside a lamp that isn't watertight.
- **Cut for precision.** I left out lamp functions the product doesn't have (fog, direction indicator, daytime running) and the R13H rules on generating the braking signal, which concern the vehicle's brakes rather than the lamp.

Two things in the source material are worth knowing:

- **Stop lamp limit.** The exploded view says the stop lamp gives 60 to 185 cd on axis. Neither regulation sets 185 cd: both cap a steady stop lamp (category S1) at 260 cd, or 130 cd if marked "D". The product's figure is within those limits.
- **R3 paragraph labels.** The R3 text file labels its last annexes oddly: production-conformity paragraphs appear under "Annex 12" even though the text calls them Annexes 17 and 18. I cited the labels exactly as they appear in the file.
